config NTUPLE
	tristate "NTUPLE - N-way Redundant File Table with Wear, Pressure & Health"
	select PROC_FS
	help
	  NTUPLE replaces a single on-disk File Table with an N-way redundant
	  set (the "multitude": 1, 3, 5, 10, ... layers; default 10). It keeps
	  three coordinated tables:

	    Table 1 (FILE)   - the file entries, replicated across N layers.
	    Table 2 (HEALTH) - per-layer and per-region read/write/press/wear and
	                       disk-health accounting ABOUT Table 1. Read-heat and
	                       write-wear accrue with access; heavy/jarring access
	                       records extra impact across the N layers.
	    Table 3 (ADMIN)  - administrative and state properties (technical id,
	                       monitor health, permits, versioning, timestamps, and
	                       operator-supplied opaque reference values).

	  The engine derives a "quality" and a "pressure" for each read against
	  the machine/device spec (device class, PCIe/NVMe ceiling, latency), so
	  the more a region is read, the more wear is appealed to and noted. The
	  redundant secondary table remains evident about the primary File Table
	  at the configured multiplicity (3x/5x/10x).

	  Exposes:
	    /proc/ntuple/status   - config, layers, disk-health summary
	    /proc/ntuple/health   - per-layer wear/pressure/quality (Table 2)
	    /proc/ntuple/admin    - administrative/state properties (Table 3)

	  ETHICS NOTE: Table 3 stores administrative facts and OPERATOR-SUPPLIED
	  opaque values only. The kernel does NOT compute, infer, or judge any
	  person's intelligence, worth, feelings, or social standing. Subjective
	  fields are stored verbatim as operator-provided reference numbers with no
	  kernel-assigned meaning, consistent with the project's stated ethics.

	  If unsure, say M.
